Description: cellpaintr tests whether a perturbation, such as a drug,
    changes the morphology of cells in Cell Painting experiments with few
    biological replicates, such as patient-derived cells from a handful of
    donors. It reads the per-cell measurement tables that CellProfiler
    exports (for example MyExpt_Cells.csv with image metadata columns) into
    a SingleCellExperiment, removes poor cells and features, transforms and
    scales the features, and corrects plate row and column effects. It then
    learns on cells and tests on replicates: a random forest trained on the
    cells of all other replicates scores the cells of each held-out
    replicate, and an exact permutation test or a corrected paired t-test
    compares the scores between conditions, for all features and for feature
    groups such as measurement types or imaging channels. Unlike profiling
    pipelines such as pycytominer, which aggregate cells into well-level
    profiles and compare their similarity, cellpaintr trains on single cells,
    so it learns the direction of an effect from the data and detects changes
    in the spread or shape of a feature distribution that leave the means
    unchanged.
